WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
The first film ended on a cost.

2
00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:07.700
A unit short of air sends on blood low in oxygen, and the mix pays for it.

3
00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:10.820
Can the lung do anything about that unit?

4
00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:19.410
In a working muscle, an arteriole short of oxygen widens and draws more blood.

5
00:00:20.690 --> 00:00:22.770
The lung's small arteries do the opposite.

6
00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:26.330
Where alveolar oxygen falls, they narrow.

7
00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:31.730
Blood leaves the poorly ventilated region instead of pooling in it.

8
00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:34.790
Follow the poorly ventilated unit.

9
00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:39.050
Its air is unchanged, but less blood reaches it.

10
00:00:40.310 --> 00:00:43.490
Ventilation over less perfusion is a larger ratio.

11
00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:46.890
So its low ratio is pushed back up.

12
00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:50.790
Its lost blood goes to better-ventilated units.

13
00:00:51.833 --> 00:00:54.793
The classic experiment came in 1946.

14
00:00:56.433 --> 00:01:01.913
Von Euler and Liljestrand ventilated anaesthetized cats by pump and recorded

15
00:01:01.913 --> 00:01:03.573
pulmonary arterial pressure.

16
00:01:04.953 --> 00:01:07.953
Ten and a half percent oxygen strongly raised it.

17
00:01:08.913 --> 00:01:10.353
Pure oxygen lowered it slightly.

18
00:01:11.483 --> 00:01:13.343
Nerves might cause such a rise.

19
00:01:14.523 --> 00:01:17.883
So they cut the vagus nerves and removed the stellate ganglia.

20
00:01:19.103 --> 00:01:21.923
If nerves carried the signal, the rise should vanish.

21
00:01:23.383 --> 00:01:24.383
Did it?

22
00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:31.760
It did not; the rise survived.

23
00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:37.700
They concluded it was due, in part, to hypoxia acting directly on the vessels.

24
00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:45.260
Keep that hedge. A pressure is not a resistance unless flow and outflow pressure are

25
00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:49.700
known. Nor can whole-lung hypoxia test regional matching.

26
00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:53.620
Still, their proposal is the idea to carry.

27
00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:58.400
Where region runs short of oxygen, its small arteries narrow.

28
00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:01.860
Blood shifts toward the better aerated parts.

29
00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:05.480
That is the lung's local correction for mismatch.

30
00:02:06.683 --> 00:02:09.163
How does a vessel respond to low oxygen?

31
00:02:10.483 --> 00:02:15.443
In outline, hypoxia inhibits an outward potassium current in the artery's smooth

32
00:02:15.443 --> 00:02:21.063
muscle. The cell depolarizes, and voltage-gated calcium channels open.

33
00:02:22.243 --> 00:02:23.883
That is one route among several.

34
00:02:24.967 --> 00:02:30.307
The sensor upstream is still hypothesized; mitochondria are the leading candidate.

35
00:02:31.627 --> 00:02:38.047
In mice lacking one mitochondrial subunit, Cox4i2, isolated lungs did not constrict

36
00:02:38.047 --> 00:02:42.807
to hypoxia. They constricted normally to potassium chloride.

37
00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:46.000
So the contraction machinery works.

38
00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:51.220
In mouse lungs, the defect sits upstream, in sensing or its signaling.

39
00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:55.460
In humans, the sensor remains an open question.

40
00:02:56.550 --> 00:03:00.890
In healthy lungs at rest, on air at sea level, the response does little.

41
00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:06.930
It matters when ventilation becomes uneven between regions, or at altitude.

42
00:03:08.033 --> 00:03:10.653
So a spread of ratios is not left alone.

43
00:03:11.893 --> 00:03:16.013
A region short of air gives up part of its blood, and its ratio rises.

44
00:03:17.493 --> 00:03:19.153
Blood is steered toward units with air.
